[center][img]https://raw.githubusercontent.com/Edgemodders/REDcode/main/docs/assets/brand/banner.png[/img][/center]

[center][size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]A Cyberpunk 2077 modding workspace in VS Code, built on WolvenKit.[/color][/b][/size][/center]

[center][size=3]Browse the game's archives, add files to a WolvenKit project, edit them in a tree and property grid, see textures, meshes in 3D and sounds, browse the TweakDB and the game's texts, then pack, install and start the game. All in one window, next to your code and your AI assistant.[/size][/center]

[line]

[quote][size=4][b][color=#fcee0a]STATUS: PREVIEW[/color][/b][/size]
What is here works and is tested against the game. New features land in small updates.
[b]This is not a game mod.[/b] It is an extension for VS Code, so install it there (steps below), not with Vortex. The quickest way is straight from the [url=https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=Edgemodders.redcode-cyberpunk]VS Code Marketplace[/url] or [url=https://open-vsx.org/extension/edgemodders/redcode-cyberpunk]Open VSX[/url]; the file here is the same extension.[/quote]

[line]

[color=#19f3ff][i][Screenshot 1: REDcode open on a project: the Project and Game Assets views on the left, a CR2W file in the tree and property grid on the right.][/i][/color]

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]What REDcode is[/color][/b][/size]

REDcode is built by [url=https://edgemodders.ai/]EDGEmodders[/url], a modding community whose idea is simple: [b]anyone can mod[/b]. It brings the game's files into VS Code, the editor most modders and AI assistants already use.

REDcode stands on [b]WolvenKit[/b]. Every game file is read and written by WolvenKit's own libraries, which run in [b]REDbridge[/b], a small local program that REDcode downloads, starts and talks to. Projects are WolvenKit's own [i].cpmodproj[/i] projects, byte for byte, so they move freely between REDcode and WolvenKit. Credit for the file formats, the archive reader, the importers and exporters, and years of research belongs to the WolvenKit team and contributors.

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Highlights[/color][/b][/size]

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]The game's archives as a tree[/color][/b][/size]
Every file in the game, in folders, with search as you type and a filter that narrows the tree while you type ([i]osprey mesh[/i], [i]ep1\weapons\*.xbm[/i]). Open any file read-only, or add it to your project for an editable copy.

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]A real CR2W editor[/color][/b][/size]
.ent, .app, .mesh, materials, scenes and the rest open as a tree and a property grid: dropdowns for enums, add and remove in arrays, undo and redo, search across the whole file. Localization files open as a table you edit in place. Atlases open on their texture with every part drawn over it, to move and resize.

[color=#19f3ff][i][Screenshot 2: the atlas editor, an .inkatlas with its parts drawn over the texture.][/i][/color]

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]Textures, meshes and sounds[/color][/b][/size]
Textures show as images. Meshes open in 3D: orbit, pick the LOD, switch appearances, turn submeshes on and off, see each one's material. A GLB shows with its own textures. [b]Edit in Blender[/b] opens a model in Blender, and every save there comes back into your project by itself. Voice lines and sound effects play in a player with a waveform, and [b]Browse Game Sounds[/b] lists the game's 73,000 sound effects as events to filter and play.

[color=#19f3ff][i][Screenshot 3: a vehicle or weapon mesh in the 3D view, with the submesh list.][/i][/color]

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]The TweakDB, browsable[/color][/b][/size]
Every record type and record, with a page per record where links take you to the records it names, LocKeys show their text and resource paths open the file. [b]New Tweak from This Record[/b] writes a TweakXL file that clones or changes it, every flat listed and ready to uncomment. Hover a record path in a tweak file to see what it is.

[color=#19f3ff][i][Screenshot 4: a TweakDB record page, for example a weapon, with its flats and links.][/i][/color]

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]The game's texts, in 19 languages[/color][/b][/size]
The [b]LocKeys[/b] view finds the text behind any [i]LocKey#[/i], and the LocKey behind any text, in the base game and Phantom Liberty. Click a result to open it in its file. One click adds it to your project's own text file, made and wired up for ArchiveXL if you have none, so you can change what the game says. Hover a LocKey in a tweak, a script or a CR2W file to read it.

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]Pack, install, play[/color][/b][/size]
[b]Pack[/b] builds your mod the way WolvenKit does (Ctrl+Shift+B), [b]Install[/b] puts it in the game (through REDdeck when you have it, so you can undo it), and [b]Run Game[/b] starts Cyberpunk. [b]Pack and Try[/b] does it in one click, straight into the running game when you have Red Hot Tools. Export to glTF, PNG and WAV, edit, and import back: a GLB can become a new mesh, and edited sound effects go back into the game's sound banks. REDmod packs carry your custom sounds.

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]TweakXL files[/color][/b][/size]
Syntax highlighting, brackets, comments and snippets for TweakXL's [i].tweak[/i] format.

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]For AI assistants[/color][/b][/size]
[url=https://www.npmjs.com/package/@edgemodders/redcode-mcp]@edgemodders/redcode-mcp[/url] gives Claude Code and other assistants the same engine: browse the archives, read and patch CR2W files, export, import, pack, and query the TweakDB and the game's texts. Ask your assistant to make the mod, and watch it work in the same project.

[size=4][b][color=#19f3ff]Coming next[/color][/b][/size]
Planned: the [b]REDnode loop[/b], to edit a REDnode mod and see it in the running game without a restart, with the game's logs and a debugger in VS Code.

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Requirements[/color][/b][/size]

[list]
[*]Windows 10 or 11, 64-bit.[/*]
[*][url=https://code.visualstudio.com/]VS Code[/url] 1.70 or later, [url=https://vscodium.com/]VSCodium[/url], or EDGEkit.[/*]
[*]Cyberpunk 2077 on PC: the Steam, GOG or Epic install. REDcode finds it.[/*]
[*]REDbridge: REDcode offers to download it on first start. Nothing to set up.[/*]
[*]The .NET 10 runtime, which REDbridge runs on. REDcode offers to install it when it is missing.[/*]
[*]Optional: [url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDdeck]REDdeck[/url], for installs you can undo.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Install[/color][/b][/size]

[b]From a store (recommended, updates itself):[/b] in VS Code, open the Extensions view (Ctrl+Shift+X), search for [b]REDcode for Cyberpunk 2077[/b] and click Install. Or open its [url=https://marketplace.visualstudio.com/items?itemName=Edgemodders.redcode-cyberpunk]Marketplace[/url] or [url=https://open-vsx.org/extension/edgemodders/redcode-cyberpunk]Open VSX[/url] page.

[b]From the file here:[/b]
[list]
[*]Download the main file with [b]Manual download[/b] and extract it anywhere.[/*]
[*]In VS Code, open the Extensions view (Ctrl+Shift+X), click [b]...[/b] at its top, choose [b]Install from VSIX...[/b] and pick [i]redcode-cyberpunk-<version>.vsix[/i].[/*]
[*]Click the REDcode icon in the activity bar. REDcode looks for the game and offers to download REDbridge.[/*]
[/list]

Do not install it with Vortex: it is not a game mod, and Vortex would put it in the game folder.

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]The EDGE platform[/color][/b][/size]

REDcode is one of the EDGE tools. Each works on its own and together:

[list]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDdeck]REDdeck[/url][/b]: a simple mod manager in VS Code. REDcode's Install goes through it.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDnode]REDnode[/url][/b]: mods in JavaScript, web pages over the game, and REDagent, an AI assistant in the game.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDbridge]REDbridge[/url][/b]: runs WolvenKit's libraries for REDcode, REDdeck and REDnode.[/*]
[*][b]The REDnode HUD theme[/b]: the dark, yellow and cyan theme REDcode's pages are drawn for, in the same stores.[/*]
[*][b][url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/EDGEkit]EDGEkit[/url][/b]: one editor with every EDGE tool built in.[/*]
[/list]

[size=5][b][color=#fcee0a]Questions[/color][/b][/size]

[b]Does it replace WolvenKit?[/b]
No. It runs WolvenKit's libraries and writes WolvenKit's projects, so you can switch between the two on the same project. Use whichever suits the task.

[b]Why is REDbridge a separate download?[/b]
WolvenKit's libraries are GPL-3.0 and run in .NET, so they live in their own program, REDbridge (GPL-3.0), which REDcode talks to. REDcode itself stays MIT. REDcode downloads REDbridge from its GitHub releases and tells you when there is a newer one.

[b]Will it change my game folder?[/b]
Only when you press Install (or Pack and Install), and only with your mod's files. Through REDdeck, an install can be undone exactly.

[b]Is it free?[/b]
Yes, and open source under the MIT license. The code is on [url=https://github.com/Edgemodders/REDcode]GitHub[/url].

